{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## RCA — 2f46 rebar-gerrit autodeploy unhealthy (2026-08-29→30, us-east-1, acct 896586841071)\n\n**Protocol:** `/rebar-debug` Phase 1 (Understand). Investigation was READ-ONLY against AWS/the Gerrit host (CloudWatch alarm-transition history + metrics; SSM `journalctl`/`docker`/`df` probes). No infra, config, or source change was made. Disposition = **STOP + ESCALATE** (every candidate fix is behavior/infra altering, not a bounded local-code defect fix). Ticket left `in_progress`, not closed.\n\n### Verdict on the (a)/(b)/(c) question\n**(a) — recurrence of an already-known, operator-ACCEPTED design limitation that has now become a problem in practice.** It is NOT a regressed fix, NOT downstream of disk bug e4f3 (b), and NOT genuinely new (c). The dedup `duplicates`→6af7 on this ticket is a **triage mis-dedup** (see below); the mechanism actually belongs to **40a7** (`surreal-horsy-skimmer`, closed `not_a_bug`).\n\n### Proven mechanism #1 — `deploy_errors ×11` + `mcp-retire-cap ×11` (same event)\n- SSM journal shows the 11 `mcp-retire-cap` and the bulk of `deploy_errors` are **time-locked to the same ticks**: `AUTODEPLOY_MCP_RETIRE_CAP reason=port-exhausted \"both 8092 and 8093 held by un-reaped mcp containers; cap=3\"` alongside `AUTODEPLOY_ERROR reason=deploy_failed component=mcp target=… fail#N backoff`. Same timestamps ⇒ one event surfacing on two alarms.\n- Root mechanism = **40a7**: `src/rebar/_mcp_health.py:743` sets uvicorn `timeout_graceful_shutdown=int(grace_seconds)` with `DEFAULT_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_SECONDS=1200` (line 51). For a Streamable-HTTP MCP server, connected clients hold long-lived streams, so uvicorn graceful shutdown burns the **full 1200 s** even when the in-flight gauge is 0. `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh` sets `MCP_STOP_GRACE=1260` (line 169) and only **two** rotation ports (`MCP_PORT_A/B=8092/8093`; 8091 reserved) with `MCP_RELEASES_CAP=3` (line 168). A retiring container pins one port for ~20 min; a second mcp deploy inside that window finds no free port → `port-exhausted` → `deploy_failed` + backoff, re-tried each ~120 s tick.\n- Runtime confirmation: container `rebar-mcp-1f1606b93965-8092` first `docker stop --time 1260` at ts 1788029283, reaped at 1788030592 = **1309 s** later (full grace + SIGKILL), i.e. it did NOT fast-drain despite 0 in-flight. Coupling is **still live in current `origin/main`**.\n- Cadence driver (what pushed an accepted ceiling over the edge): in-window there were **14 distinct mcp deploy targets** and **142 review-bot redeploys** (139 deferrals) — a high-cadence landing burst. 14 mcp rotations through a 2-port pool with ~20 min port-hold each is exactly the 40a7 congestion regime, at scale, for the first time.\n- **40a7 was closed `not_a_bug` by explicit OPERATOR DECISION**: \"mcp deploys capped ~1/20min whenever a client holds a session; **if that ceiling ever becomes a problem in practice, reopen this ticket.**\" That condition is now met (11 alarm cycles in one burst).\n\n### Proven mechanism #2 — `review-interrupts signal-unavailable ×2`\n- Emitted by `autodeploy.sh:846` (`marker AUTODEPLOY_REVIEW_INTERRUPT signal-unavailable`) when `bot_in_flight_reviews()` (line 283) returns **-1** — curl to `http://localhost:8000/health` failed / `in_flight` unparseable — AND a review-bot deploy is triggered. The bot is then recreated **fail-open, without the drain check** (the marker exists precisely to make that blind deploy observable).\n- Raw markers have distinct real timestamps ~daily ~16:50–17:05 PDT across 08-28/29/30 (1787961940, 1788047355/1788047692, 1788133956), and the metric datapoints are published at those real times — so these are **genuine fail-opens, NOT the e2a6 cold-start republish** (interrupt-signal offset file present = 5, not absent; e2a6 RULED OUT).\n- On 08-29 the firing coincides with `bot-build-failed` (ts 1788047559): the review-bot was down/rebuilding (its own `compose build/up review-bot` takes `/health` down), and a subsequent tick read `in_flight=-1`. The daily regularity points at a periodic bot rebuild/unreachability window, driven by the same high redeploy cadence (142 bot redeploys in-window).\n\n### Hypothesis (b) — disk bug e4f3 — REJECTED as the cause (decisive timing)\n- e4f3's ticket comments were empty in-store, so I used CloudWatch `root-disk-pressure` alarm transitions directly. Windows (PDT):\n  - `mcp-retire-cap`: ALL 11 between **08-29 14:50 → 08-30 03:06**.\n  - `deploy_errors`: 10 of 11 on **08-29** (14:50→22:41); 1 on 08-30 16:58.\n  - `root-disk-pressure` (e4f3): first sustained in-window flap **08-30 10:58**, then 11:53 / 12:27 / 15:43 / 16:47 — i.e. **entirely AFTER retire-cap had stopped (03:06)**.\n- Effect cannot precede cause: disk pressure that begins 08-30 10:58 cannot have driven retire-cap/deploy_errors that ran 08-29 14:50→08-30 03:06. The dominant ×11/×11 pattern is 40a7 and PREDATES the disk flaps. ⇒ 2f46 is a **distinct concurrent issue, not downstream of e4f3** — do NOT defer/merge/double-fix.\n- The **only** plausible disk contribution is the single 08-30 ~16:50 cluster (the lone 08-30 16:58 `deploy_errors` + the 08-30 16:53 `signal-unavailable`, both ~minutes after disk ALARM 16:47). Even those have an independent explanation (40a7 congestion; daily bot-rebuild unreachability). Note it as a secondary, partial overlap — not the cause.\n\n### Also ruled out\n- **6af7 recurrence:** 6af7 = `AuthConfigError`/secrets-not-refreshed (fixed via Gerrit 2230), a DIFFERENT `deploy_failed` cause. No `AuthConfigError`/secrets markers appear in-window. The `duplicates`→6af7 link is a mis-dedup; the correct mechanism ticket is 40a7.\n\n### Attribution (origin of the mechanism)\n- `git blame src/rebar/_mcp_health.py:743` → commit **8297083532a3f6f87754f34eb64f010e8d14e8f0** (\"Add rebar-mcp HTTP service to the AWS box\", 2026-08-24), which carries `rebar-ticket: fd4a-4e91-f179-4932` (story \"rebar-mcp HTTP transport service on the AWS box\"). This introduced `DEFAULT_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_SECONDS=1200` and the uvicorn-grace coupling.\n- This matches 40a7's own attribution: 40a7 had a `caused_by`→fd4a edge and **removed it on its `not_a_bug` close, with a note to restore it if reopened**. I am deliberately NOT writing a `caused_by` edge here because the defect *legitimacy* is exactly what is being escalated (40a7 judged this intended behavior). Recommended: if the operator reopens 40a7 as a defect, restore `caused_by 40a7 → fd4a` (and/or `2f46 → fd4a`) at that point.\n\n### Escalation (why this stops at Phase 1)\nEvery candidate fix is behavior/infra-altering — NOT a bounded, reversible, local-code defect fix — so per the skill this must be escalated, not auto-applied:\n1. **Decouple** uvicorn `timeout_graceful_shutdown` from the certified-op grace (e.g. fast-drain to a short shutdown once the in-flight gauge hits 0, instead of always burning 1200 s). Blast radius: changes MCP shutdown semantics; risks cutting a genuinely-draining op short.\n2. **Expand** the blue-green mcp port pool / raise `MCP_RELEASES_CAP`. Blast radius: infra/config (nginx upstreams, firewalling, reserved ports).\n3. **Distinguish** \"bot mid-redeploy\" (expected transient `/health` outage) from \"bot wedged\" before fail-open recreation, so signal-unavailable stops firing on normal redeploys. Blast radius: changes the drain-gate decision logic.\n\n**Recommended tracker actions (for operator):** reopen 40a7 (its reopen condition is now met); re-point 2f46's dedup from 6af7 to 40a7; treat e4f3 as a separate concurrent P2.\n\n*Evidence base: CloudWatch alarm-transition histories + metric datapoints for all four alarms; read-only SSM `journalctl`/`docker`/`df` probes on i-00880b2c7f13527c5; git blame of `_mcp_health.py:743`. Investigation READ-ONLY throughout; no mutations applied.*"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788143563957900674,"uuid":"06ee8db7-0842-4b97-8ffb-2bb2c12fd3c2"}